Have had a quick play with this...
> I had a couple of ideas about the g_free problem, but haven't had a chance to try them yet.
> 
>1. Try and resolve the g_free relative virtual address by inspecting the qemu binary, add that to the base address that qemu is loaded at and cast it to a fptr and use that. Not sure if I'll be able to do this, will require sufficient debug info in qemu.
> 
This is a non-starter, qemu as downloaded doesn't have debug symbols: 
![image](https://github.com/novafacing/qemu-rs/assets/1396183/e8ddbe1b-0d61-46f3-8c6e-c0b840c1c01e)

>2. I think that on windows glib ends up just calling the 'normal' free function you get from Windows. If so, we might get away with calling that directly. If this worked, we would be relying on glib never changing internal details of his it mallocs and frees on windows.
This could work:
```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
unsafe fn g_free(mem: *mut c_void){
    //HACK: We would really like to call g_free in the qemu binary here
    //but we can't, because windows doesn't export symbols unless you explicitly export them
    //and qemu doesn't export g_free.

    //Instead we'll rely on glib only using stdlib.h's malloc/free and call it directly.
    //On glib docs, it says
    //> Since GLib 2.46 g_malloc() is hardcoded to always use the system malloc implementation.
    //So should be safe to call free ourselves.
    unsafe {
        if !mem.is_null() {
            libc::free(mem);
        }
    }
}
````

Ah interesting, it seems that the "Flags" in the true sense (bitwise combinable) doesn't exist yet in the API? From a quick check, although "CallbackFlags" has "Flags" in the name, its actually just a "mutually exclusive" enum value (of e.g. CPU_NO_REG, R_REG, and RW_REGS).

Some investigation, the `bitflags` crate is able to integrate & generate with bindgen? Not sure this is perfect, but usage looks nice?

```rust
use qemu_plugin::{DisconFlags, PluginId, qemu_plugin_register_vcpu_discon_cb};

// Register for all discontinuity events
qemu_plugin_register_vcpu_discon_cb(
    plugin_id,
    DisconFlags::ALL,
    Some(my_callback),
)?;

// Register for specific events
let flags = DisconFlags::INTERRUPT | DisconFlags::EXCEPTION;
qemu_plugin_register_vcpu_discon_cb(plugin_id, flags, Some(my_callback))?;
```

Implementation would require additional dependency on `bitflags` crate (without optional, so just bitflags itself):
```rust
// Bitflags for discontinuity event types (v6+)
#[cfg(feature = "plugin-api-v6")]
pub mod discon_flags {
    use bitflags::bitflags;

    bitflags! {
        /// Flags for discontinuity event types
        ///
        /// These flags can be combined using bitwise OR to register callbacks
        /// for multiple discontinuity event types at once.
        #[repr(transparent)]
        #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
        pub struct DisconFlags: i32 {
            /// An interrupt - defined across all architectures as an asynchronous
            /// event, usually originating from outside the CPU
            const INTERRUPT = 1;

            /// An exception - defined across all architectures as a synchronous
            /// event in response to a specific instruction being executed
            const EXCEPTION = 2;

            /// A host call - functionally a special kind of exception that is not
            /// handled by code run by the vCPU but machinery outside the vCPU
            const HOSTCALL = 4;

            /// All types of discontinuity events currently covered
            const ALL = -1;
        }
    }

    impl Default for DisconFlags {
        fn default() -> Self {
            Self::empty()
        }
    }
}

#[cfg(feature = "plugin-api-v6")]
pub use discon_flags::DisconFlags;
```

