I share your opinion that a Rust API full of unsafe and c_void types is not what we want to offer in the end. To be honest, if the Rust API is successful and natural, I would even be happy to call it the main language for plugins. Writing plugins dealing with data structures in C is just a pain.

For the callbacks, I played a bit with Rust yesterday, and that's what we could offer for native callbacks within the constraints I mentioned. It uses closures (without any environment), but normal functions could be used also.

```rust
// this function is what we would get from QEMU on C side. It's just implemented in Rust here
// for convenience of having a single file example.
// 
// extern void register_callback_c(cb_type cb, void* userdata)
extern "C"
fn register_callback_c(cb: *mut c_void, userdata: *const c_void) {
    let f: fn(&mut Data) = unsafe { std::mem::transmute(cb) };
    let data: &mut Data = unsafe { &mut *(userdata as *mut Data) };
    f(data);
}

// That's the Rust interface we want to offer: One generic function per register_* on C side,
// to ensure type consistency between callback and data passed, and hide c_void details.
fn register_callback<T>(cb: fn(&mut T), userdata: &mut T) {
    register_callback_c(cb as *mut c_void, userdata as *const T as *const c_void)
}

// This structure is where the Plugin state will be kept, It's passed by mutable reference
// to all callbacks.
pub struct Data {
    str: String,
}

fn main() {
    // In practice we would allocate this on the heap, and keep a raw pointer to it.
    // We'll free it from exit callback, similar to what C++ example does above.
    let mut msg = Data {
        str: String::from("Hello World"),
    };
    register_callback(
        |s: &mut Data| {
            s.str.push_str("!");
            println!("{}", s.str);
            register_callback(|s: &mut String| println!("{}", s), &mut s.str);
        },
        &mut msg,
    );
    println!("after: {}", msg.str);
}
```

What I didn't cover is how to pass back a `tb` or `insn` pointer to our callback, but I think we could have something like:
```
struct TranslationBlock {
  from_qemu: c_void,
}
```
This way, we can implement all the functions on TranslationBlock, that will simply transmute the pointer coming from QEMU.

Finally, for the glib types, when treated as read-only, we can probably hack something to reproduce structure of GArray and GByteArray (just a pointer + len). For the read-write or write-data, we can probably pay for the dynamic allocation where needed, and offer only normal Vec or array on rust side.

And a bigger example with the idea to plumb pointer to C under Rust structure.
Full code is in tar.gz attached.

```rust
use std::ffi::c_void;

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// API directly exported from QEMU
extern "C" {
    // Implemented in src/register_callback.c
    fn register_callback_c(
        cb: extern "C" fn(TranslationBlock, *const c_void),
        userdata: *mut c_void,
    );

    fn translation_block_addr(tb: *const c_void) -> u64;
    fn translation_block_length(tb: *const c_void) -> u16;
}

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Our high level API

// We abstract C type by a structure having a ptr, Use transparent to ensure layout is the same as
// the underlying type when there is a single field. This allows to have native Rust callbacks
// taking this type as value, to abstract that C API sends an internal pointer.
#[repr(transparent)]
struct TranslationBlock {
    ptr: *const c_void,
}

// Implement methods for TranslationBlock
impl TranslationBlock {
    fn addr(&self) -> u64 {
        unsafe { translation_block_addr(self.ptr) }
    }

    fn length(&self) -> u16 {
        unsafe { translation_block_length(self.ptr) }
    }
}

// One generic wrapper per callback register. It ensures we have a matching between callbacktype
// and userdata.
fn register_callback<T>(cb: fn(TranslationBlock, &mut T), userdata: &mut T) {
    unsafe {
        register_callback_c(
            std::mem::transmute(cb),
            userdata as *mut T as *mut c_void,
        )
    }
}

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Plugin code

pub struct Data {
    str: String,
}

fn main() {
    let mut msg = Data {
        str: String::from("Hello World"),
    };
    register_callback(
        |tb: TranslationBlock, s: &mut Data| {
            s.str.push_str("!");
            println!("{} (addr={:#x}, length={})", s.str, tb.addr(), tb.length());
        },
        &mut msg,
    );
    println!("after: {}", msg.str);
```

