I didn't notice at first but the `register_callback` fn will not be safe this way. We do not make sure that the referenced data lives until the callbacks happen. "Just" requesting users to take care of that in documentation is not an option. So we need something like:

```
fn register_callback<T: Sync>(cb: fn(TranslationBlock, &T), userdata: T) {
    let userdata = Box::new(userdata);
    // TODO: register userdata for cleanup on TB flush
    unsafe {
        register_callback_c(
            std::mem::transmute(cb),
            userdata.into_raw() as *mut c_void,
        )
    }
}
```

We would likely end up with an `Arc` since that makes cleanup easier. Ideally we would want `Box::as_mut_ptr`[^1] for the callback and the `Box` itself stuffed into the bookkeeping.

The entire bookkeeping is probably not something we want as a "mid"`-level QEMU API. So maybe `register_callback` fns should not accept a reference or value but a (typed) ptr after all and leave the rest to higher level APIs. Or take something like an `Arc` and do some hackery with `Weak` references.

[^1]: that's still unstable, but will be stable in the foreseeable future (stabilization PR was merged already)
